package ui

// The empty, error and degraded states of UI §4.7, plus the primitives that
// state copy is set in.
//
// A state is a designed surface using the full shell, not a branch inside a
// page: §4.7 says so, and it is also what makes the contract assertable. Each
// one here is a single-parameter component with its own accessible name, its
// own `data-testid`, and the record's own copy wherever the record supplies any.
// A handler chooses which state to render and fills in what only it knows; the
// wording lives here, because a sentence written in a handler is a sentence
// nobody reads twice and a vocabulary test cannot reach (UI §1.2, §10.2).
//
// THE ELEVEN, AND WHERE THE ELEVENTH COMES FROM
// ---------------------------------------------
// §4.7's table has ten rows: first run, no pages, search without a query, search
// without results, 403, 404, 412, 500, an unknown `system_id`, and
// `ruleset_version` drift. §4.7's own task list and §15.3 step 8 both say
// eleven. The eleventh is `ConnectionLost`: §7.5's live-region table lists
// "Ruleset drift, game ended, connection lost" and cites §4.7 for all three, and
// of the two that the table does not design, connection loss is the one with
// state behind it — the overview's §7 defines reconnect, and UI §9 lists mass
// disconnect as a handled failure. So the eleven are the ten rows plus that one.
//
// "Game ended" is the twelfth §7.5 attributes to §4.7 and is deliberately NOT
// here. There is no domain state behind it — the architecture plan's intent set
// has no such op — and UI §4.9 says, about exactly this, "if turn-end is not an
// op, the UI cannot ship without a plan amendment; flag it, do not invent a
// client-side turn flag". A state whose only content would be an invented flag
// is worse than an absent one, so it waits for the amendment.
//
// NOTHING HERE IS A LIVE REGION EXCEPT FOUR STATES
// ------------------------------------------------
// §7.5 fixes which announcements are assertive and which are polite, and
// §13.4 requires the search route to carry none at all. A live region is not a
// way to make a page louder: a `role="alert"` fires when content is *inserted*
// into it, so a region that was in the document at load says nothing, and a
// region a handler re-renders on every navigation says something every time. The
// four states that do carry `role="alert"` are the ones the record names for it:
// a 412 conflict, ruleset drift, and a lost connection — plus, in phase 10, a
// secret reveal and a capped reconciliation, neither of which exists yet. The
// other seven states are regions a reader reaches by navigating, and navigating
// is not an event.

import (
	"strings"
	"unicode/utf8"
)

// Heading says which heading element a state renders its own heading as.
type Heading int

const (
	// HeadingInPage is the zero value: a route already owns the page's `<h1>`,
	// and the state renders its heading as `<h2>` beneath it. The zero value
	// because that is what `components.CampaignList` and `components.WikiArticle`
	// already do, so a route that mounts a state into its centre slot does not
	// have to think about it and cannot get it wrong by omission.
	HeadingInPage Heading = iota
	// HeadingPage: the state is the whole centre, so it owns the `<h1>`. An error
	// page has no other heading to put one in, and UI §7.5 requires a search
	// result count in the `<h1>` — "12 results for goblin" — so the two search
	// states need it. Five of the eleven are rendered this way in their
	// fixtures; the field is per-state rather than per-package because the two
	// search states and a 404 are mounted by different routes with different
	// surroundings.
	HeadingPage
)

// stateHeading renders a state's own heading at the level its placement calls
// for.
//
// One helper rather than a branch in eleven templates: "heading levels never
// skip" (UI §7.2) is a rule about the document, and eleven copies of the same
// branch is eleven chances to spell it differently. The two states whose heading
// contains markup the record specifies — a path in a 404, the query term in a
// search result count — branch inline, because a helper that took a component
// would have to be handed a no-op for the other nine.
templ stateHeading(id, text string, heading Heading) {
	if heading == HeadingPage {
		<h1 id={ id }>{ text }</h1>
	} else {
		<h2 id={ id }>{ text }</h2>
	}
}

// headingID derives a state's heading element id from its own test hook.
//
// Derived rather than supplied because a caller inventing one would invent a
// different one per render, and an `aria-labelledby` naming an id that moves is a
// region with no accessible name. The test hook is the one string per state that
// is already unique in a document, which makes it the only id source that
// cannot produce a duplicate.
func headingID(testID string) string {
	return testID + "-heading"
}

// displayText bounds a caller-supplied string before it is rendered.
//
// Every value a caller passes into user-visible copy is attacker-reachable or
// operator-supplied: a request id from a header, a page path from a URL, a query
// term from a query string, a system id from a campaign's own configuration.
// Bounded because §4.7 requires the first three to be *echoed* — a load failure
// stays diagnosable because the reader can quote its reference — and an
// unbounded echo of a 40kB query string is a denial of service on the reader's
// own screen. `ToValidUTF8` first so a truncated value cannot end mid-rune, and
// the cut walks back to a rune boundary for the same reason: a lone continuation
// byte renders as U+FFFD in some engines and as nothing in others.
//
// The bound is a display bound and not a validation one. It never decides
// whether a value is legitimate, only how much of it is shown.
func displayText(value string, max int) string {
	value = strings.ToValidUTF8(value, "")
	if len(value) <= max {
		return value
	}

	cut := max
	for cut > 0 && !utf8.RuneStart(value[cut]) {
		cut--
	}

	return value[:cut] + "…"
}

// The display bounds displayText is called with, one per kind of value rather
// than one for all of them. They are distinct because the things have different
// jobs: a reference is quoted verbatim into a support thread and must not be
// shortened, a query is re-edited into the field it came from and stays legible
// at 200, a page path is read once and is the longest thing a reader may be shown,
// and a name appears in a heading where a hundred and twenty characters is already
// past the point of reading one.
const (
	maxReferenceDisplay = 64
	maxQueryDisplay     = 200
	maxPathDisplay      = 256
	maxSystemIDDisplay  = 64
	maxNameDisplay      = 120
	// maxCellDisplay bounds a table cell's text, which arrives from a campaign's
	// own Markdown and can be a paragraph.
	maxCellDisplay = 320
)

// The state test hooks. Named so a caller, a test and the heading id derived
// from them all read the same string, and so the two hooks this package inherited
// from `components` — `first-run` and `error-state` — keep their existing
// spelling rather than being renamed for tidiness.

// firstRunTestID is the first-run state's hook. Inherited from `components` so
// the phase-2 render test keeps passing unchanged.
const firstRunTestID = "first-run"

// noPagesTestID is the campaign-with-no-pages state's hook.
const noPagesTestID = "state-campaign-no-pages"

// searchIdleTestID is the search-without-a-query state's hook.
const searchIdleTestID = "state-search-idle"

// searchEmptyTestID is the search-without-results state's hook.
const searchEmptyTestID = "state-search-no-results"

// forbiddenTestID is the 403 state's hook.
const forbiddenTestID = "state-error-403"

// notFoundTestID is the 404 state's hook.
const notFoundTestID = "state-error-404"

// conflictTestID is the 412 state's hook.
const conflictTestID = "state-error-412"

// loadErrorTestID is the 500 state's hook. Inherited from `components` so the
// phase-2 render test keeps passing unchanged.
const loadErrorTestID = "error-state"

// systemMissingTestID is the unknown-`system_id` state's hook.
const systemMissingTestID = "state-system-missing"

// rulesetDriftTestID is the `ruleset_version` drift state's hook.
const rulesetDriftTestID = "state-ruleset-drift"

// connectionLostTestID is the lost-connection state's hook. §7.5 cites §4.7 for
// it; see the header of this file for why it is here and "game ended" is not.
const connectionLostTestID = "state-connection-lost"

// FirstRunView is the first-run state: no campaigns exist for this account.
type FirstRunView struct {
	// CreateHref is where the call to action points. Empty omits the action,
	// leaving the explanation: a CTA pointing at a route that does not exist is
	// the one button a new operator will press.
	CreateHref string
	// DocsHref is the documentation the state offers. Empty omits the link, for
	// an operator who has not configured one.
	DocsHref string
	// Heading is the heading level. A route whose centre slot this fills owns
	// the `<h1>`; leave it at HeadingInPage.
	Heading Heading
}

// FirstRun is the centre slot when the account is a member of no campaigns.
//
// A user can exist with zero campaigns — the first account is provisioned by
// `semiplane admin create` before any browser reaches the server — so this is a
// *campaign* empty state and not a user one (UI §4.6). The copy is two lines of
// fact and one action, because §4.7 is explicit that this is not a marketing
// page: a reader standing at an empty instance has already decided to use it.
templ FirstRun(view FirstRunView) {
	<section class="empty" aria-labelledby={ headingID(firstRunTestID) } data-testid={ firstRunTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(firstRunTestID), "No campaigns yet", view.Heading)
		<p>A campaign holds the pages, the assets and the table for one game. Everything in semiplane belongs to one.</p>
		<p>Everything you add stays on this instance.</p>
		if view.CreateHref != "" {
			<a class={ TargetClass + " button button--primary" } href={ view.CreateHref } data-testid="first-run-cta">Register your first campaign</a>
		}
		if view.DocsHref != "" {
			<a class={ TargetClass } href={ view.DocsHref } data-testid="first-run-docs">Read the documentation</a>
		}
	</section>
}

// NoPagesView is the campaign-with-no-pages state.
type NoPagesView struct {
	// CreateHref is where "Create a page" points. Empty omits the control
	// entirely, which is what a player gets: UI §4.4 requires edit affordances
	// to be *absent* for a player rather than disabled, so there is nothing in
	// the DOM for them to find.
	CreateHref string
	// Heading is the heading level.
	Heading Heading
}

// NoPages is the centre slot for a campaign whose content root holds no pages.
//
// The state says what is missing and, for the reader who can fix it, the one
// control that fixes it. §4.7 gives this state a roster in the rail and an empty
// outline beside it; both belong to the route's rail slot, not to the state, and
// an empty outline panel is better than no outline panel because §7.2 forbids a
// shell that can render a landmark with nothing in it.
templ NoPages(view NoPagesView) {
	<section class="empty" aria-labelledby={ headingID(noPagesTestID) } data-testid={ noPagesTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(noPagesTestID), "This campaign has no pages yet", view.Heading)
		<p>Pages hold the notes, places and rules this campaign runs on.</p>
		if view.CreateHref != "" {
			<a class={ TargetClass + " button button--primary" } href={ view.CreateHref } data-testid="campaign-no-pages-cta">Create a page</a>
		}
	</section>
}

// SearchIdleView is the search route before a query has been submitted.
type SearchIdleView struct {
	// Action is where the form submits. `GET`, because §7.5 makes search
	// submit-to-navigate over ordinary HTTP so the response is ETag-cacheable
	// and the URL is shareable.
	Action string
	// Value is the field's current contents, echoed so a submitted-and-refined
	// reader is not asked to type it again.
	Value string
	// Heading is the heading level. §7.5 puts the result count in the `<h1>`, so
	// this state renders its own: it is the whole page.
	Heading Heading
}

// SearchIdle is the centre slot for the search route with no query.
//
// The field is focused, because §4.7 designs this state as "search field,
// focused, with a hint" — a reader who arrived at /search has already decided
// to search, and a field they have to click first is a step with no purpose.
//
// The form is a landmark, and it is labelled. §7.2's rule is about *unlabelled*
// duplicates, and a search landmark with a name is the one landmark whose whole
// job is to be found by name. `autofocus` is the only focus movement in this
// package: §7.4 prohibits a background event moving focus, and arriving on the
// search route is not one.
templ SearchIdle(view SearchIdleView) {
	<section class="empty" aria-labelledby={ headingID(searchIdleTestID) } data-testid={ searchIdleTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(searchIdleTestID), "Search", view.Heading)
		@searchForm("search-idle", view.Action, view.Value, true)
	</section>
}

// SearchEmptyView is the search route with a query and no matching pages.
type SearchEmptyView struct {
	// Action is where the form submits, exactly as in SearchIdleView.
	Action string
	// Query is the term that was searched for, echoed into the heading §7.5
	// requires and back into the field so the reader can edit rather than retype.
	Query string
	// Heading is the heading level.
	Heading Heading
}

// SearchEmpty is the centre slot for a search that matched nothing.
//
// §7.5's rule that a search route carries no live region at all is why the query
// is echoed in the heading rather than announced: the reader submitted it, so
// they know it, and a polite announcement of the reader's own keystroke is the
// noise §13.4 prohibits. The heading carries the term because §7.5 puts the
// count there, and a count of zero is still the answer to "what did you find".
templ SearchEmpty(view SearchEmptyView) {
	<section class="empty" aria-labelledby={ headingID(searchEmptyTestID) } data-testid={ searchEmptyTestID }>
		// Not stateHeading: §7.5's own example emphasises the term, and the
		// heading is the page's <h1> on this route.
		if view.Heading == HeadingPage {
			<h1 id={ headingID(searchEmptyTestID) }>No results for <em data-testid="search-no-results-term">{ displayText(view.Query, maxQueryDisplay) }</em></h1>
		} else {
			<h2 id={ headingID(searchEmptyTestID) }>No results for <em data-testid="search-no-results-term">{ displayText(view.Query, maxQueryDisplay) }</em></h2>
		}
		<p>Nothing in this campaign matches. Try fewer words, or a different spelling.</p>
		@searchForm("search-no-results", view.Action, view.Query, false)
	</section>
}

// searchForm renders the search field, its hint and its submit button.
//
// Shared by the two search states because it is one form with two starting
// points, and two copies is two chances for the ids to drift — a `<label for>`
// pointing at a field that is not there renders a field with no name, which is
// the failure §7.7 opens on.
//
// prefix namespaces the ids and the test hooks because both states may be
// rendered by a test or a preview side by side, and a duplicate id would make
// the second form's label address the first form's field.
templ searchForm(prefix, action, value string, focus bool) {
	<form class="stack form" method="get" action={ action } role="search" aria-label="Search this campaign">
		<div class="field">
			<label for={ prefix + "-query" }>Search this campaign</label>
			<input
				id={ prefix + "-query" }
				class={ TargetClass }
				type="search"
				name="q"
				value={ displayText(value, maxQueryDisplay) }
				aria-describedby={ prefix + "-hint" }
				if focus {
					autofocus
				}
				data-testid={ prefix + "-query" }
			/>
			<p id={ prefix + "-hint" } class="field-hint" data-testid={ prefix + "-hint" }>Type a word or two, then submit. Results are counted from this campaign's pages.</p>
		</div>
		<button type="submit" class={ TargetClass + " button button--primary" } data-testid={ prefix + "-submit" }>Search</button>
	</form>
}

// ForbiddenView is the 403 state.
type ForbiddenView struct {
	// CampaignName is the campaign the reader may read but not act in. §4.7
	// requires it: "so the message is specific". Naming it is safe because
	// reaching a capability gate at all means the campaign is not itself a
	// secret — ADR 0024: it is public, or the reader is a member.
	CampaignName string
	// Heading is the heading level. A 403 is a whole page, so this is normally
	// HeadingPage.
	Heading Heading
}

// Forbidden is the centre slot for a reader who can see a campaign and may not do
// what they asked for.
//
// This state is only ever rendered when the campaign is *known* to the reader.
// An anonymous request that fails a gate gets 401 and this same wording, and a
// request for a private campaign the reader cannot see gets the 404 below — see
// ADR 0024, which settles that "no access is 404, not 403" and that a 403 says
// "you are known and you may not". Rendering this state for a campaign the
// reader cannot see would turn one status code into a campaign-existence oracle,
// which is the exact leak the record forbids.
templ Forbidden(view ForbiddenView) {
	<section class="empty" aria-labelledby={ headingID(forbiddenTestID) } data-testid={ forbiddenTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(forbiddenTestID), "You do not have access to this campaign", view.Heading)
		<p>Campaign: <strong data-testid="error-403-campaign">{ displayText(view.CampaignName, maxNameDisplay) }</strong></p>
		<p>Ask a Game Master if you need access.</p>
	</section>
}

// NotFoundView is the 404 state.
//
// Three fields and no more, and that is a security property rather than
// tidiness. §4.7's closing rule is that "a 404 and a private campaign must not
// be distinguishable to an anonymous user… Both return the same body shape", so
// there is deliberately no field here that could say *why* the page is missing —
// not a reason, not a status, not a boolean. A caller with a reason in its hands
// has somewhere to put it that is not this component, and the reflection test
// `TestNotFoundViewCarriesNoReason` is what holds the line.
type NotFoundView struct {
	// PagePath is the campaign-relative page path the reader asked for, so the
	// message can name it. It is not a filesystem path and must not be one; see
	// pagePathForDisplay.
	PagePath string
	// CampaignRootHref is where the link back to the campaign goes. Empty omits
	// the link, which is the right answer only for a request outside any
	// campaign.
	CampaignRootHref string
	// Heading is the heading level.
	Heading Heading
}

// NotFound is the centre slot for a path that does not resolve.
//
// The same component serves "no page at this path" and "no campaign you can see
// at this slug", because the two must be indistinguishable — see NotFoundView.
// There is no branch on the reason, and there is nowhere to put one.
templ NotFound(view NotFoundView) {
	<section class="empty" aria-labelledby={ headingID(notFoundTestID) } data-testid={ notFoundTestID }>
		// Not stateHeading: §4.7 quotes the heading as "No page at *path*", and
		// a path in a heading reads as a path only inside a <code>.
		if view.Heading == HeadingPage {
			<h1 id={ headingID(notFoundTestID) }>No page at <code data-testid="error-404-path">{ pagePathForDisplay(view.PagePath) }</code></h1>
		} else {
			<h2 id={ headingID(notFoundTestID) }>No page at <code data-testid="error-404-path">{ pagePathForDisplay(view.PagePath) }</code></h2>
		}
		<p>It may have been renamed, or the link that brought you here may be out of date.</p>
		if view.CampaignRootHref != "" {
			<a class={ TargetClass } href={ view.CampaignRootHref } data-testid="error-404-campaign-link">Open the campaign overview</a>
		}
	</section>
}

// pagePathForDisplay decides what a 404 may echo.
//
// §4.7 says "never a raw server path echo", and the reason is not politeness: a
// filesystem path on an error page tells an anonymous reader how the operator
// laid out the disk, and a caller that reached into a route by hand could pass
// one. So the value is accepted only when it looks like a page path inside a
// campaign — relative, no traversal, no backslash, no control character — and
// otherwise the heading simply has no path in it. Losing the path on a 404 is a
// small cost; leaking the content root is the one ADR 0024 exists to prevent.
//
// A colon is *not* rejected: a page may legitimately be called "Boss: The
// Warden", the value is rendered as escaped text rather than as an address, and
// a leading slash plus the absence of backslashes already cover the absolute and
// Windows-drive cases that matter.
func pagePathForDisplay(path string) string {
	if !isPagePath(path) {
		return ""
	}

	return displayText(path, maxPathDisplay)
}

// isPagePath reports whether a value is safe to echo as a page path.
func isPagePath(path string) bool {
	if path == "" || len(path) > maxPathDisplay {
		return false
	}

	if strings.HasPrefix(path, "/") || strings.Contains(path, `\`) {
		return false
	}

	for _, segment := range strings.Split(path, "/") {
		// `..` and `.` are the two segments that mean something other than a
		// page name, and `pagePathForDisplay` is not the place that resolves
		// them — os.Root is (AGENTS.md). A path carrying them has not been
		// through path confinement, which is itself the finding.
		if segment == ".." || segment == "." {
			return false
		}
	}

	for _, character := range path {
		if character < 0x20 || character == 0x7f {
			return false
		}
	}

	return true
}

// ConflictView is the 412 state.
type ConflictView struct {
	// Heading is the heading level. This state replaces the editor's preview
	// pane, which sits under the editor's own heading, so HeadingInPage is the
	// normal value.
	Heading Heading
}

// ConflictNotice frames the 412 conflict diff in the editor's preview slot.
//
// Not an error page and not a dialog. §4.7 says the diff goes in the editor's
// preview slot, and §7.7 says explicitly "never a modal: the editor is already a
// split view, so a modal hides one side of the comparison". A modal here would
// hide the buffer the reader is being asked to reconcile against, which is the
// one thing the state exists to show side by side.
//
// `role="alert"` is the record's: §7.5 puts a 412 on save at assertive. It
// announces because this element is *inserted* into the editor — a region that
// was already in the document when the editor loaded says nothing, which is why
// the alert is on the frame rather than on the whole editor.
//
// diff is the caller's, because the two-column diff with per-hunk accept/reject
// is §4.8's and arrives in phase 6. Rendering it is the only thing this
// component adds around it, and it renders nothing at all when it is nil so the
// frame is testable before the diff exists.
templ ConflictNotice(view ConflictView, diff templ.Component) {
	<section class="notice notice--error" role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-labelledby={ headingID(conflictTestID) } data-testid={ conflictTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(conflictTestID), "This page changed while you were editing", view.Heading)
		<p data-testid="error-412-message">The saved copy is newer than the one you opened. Nothing you typed has been discarded.</p>
		if diff != nil {
			@diff
		}
	</section>
}

// LoadFailure describes a page that could not be loaded (UI §4.7's `500` row).
//
// A value rather than a sentence, for the same reason as `components.LoginFailure`:
// the wording is the interface's, and the only thing a caller contributes is a
// reference. That reference is what keeps a load failure diagnosable without a
// 500 page's worth of detail — it is the request id from the RequestID
// middleware, it ties a reader's report to a line in the access log, and it is
// safe to render because the server minted it and it names nothing.
//
// `components.LoadFailure` is declared as an alias of this type, so a route that
// builds the phase-2 shape keeps compiling and a second answer to "what does a
// failed load look like" never exists.
type LoadFailure struct {
	// Reference is the request id. Rendered only when set, and rendered through
	// displayText: a request id that a caller sourced from a header is
	// attacker-influenced, and it must not be able to fill the page.
	Reference string
	// Heading is the heading level.
	Heading Heading
}

// LoadError is the centre slot when a page could not be produced at all.
//
// §4.7's rail for this state is empty, and that is the design rather than an
// omission: the rail already names which instance and which build failed, so
// repeating either is noise. The reference line is the whole content. A reader
// who cannot load a page needs to be able to say *which request* failed without
// being shown why it failed, and the difference between those two is the
// difference between a supportable bug and an information disclosure.
templ LoadError(failure LoadFailure) {
	<section class="empty" aria-labelledby={ headingID(loadErrorTestID) } data-testid={ loadErrorTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(loadErrorTestID), "This page could not be loaded", failure.Heading)
		<p data-testid="error-state-message">semiplane could not load this page. Nothing has been changed.</p>
		if failure.Reference != "" {
			<p class="empty-note">Reference: <code data-testid="error-state-reference">{ displayText(failure.Reference, maxReferenceDisplay) }</code></p>
		}
	</section>
}

// SystemMissingView is the unknown-`system_id` state.
type SystemMissingView struct {
	// SystemID is the gameplay system's identifier, as the campaign recorded it.
	// Rendered as escaped text inside a <code>: a campaign's own configuration
	// is attacker-reachable (AGENTS.md, on Obsidian Sync), so it is bounded and
	// never interpreted.
	SystemID string
	// Heading is the heading level. This notice sits on the campaign overview,
	// under the overview's own heading.
	Heading Heading
}

// SystemMissing is the persistent notice on a campaign whose gameplay system is
// not installed.
//
// §4.7 is precise about the consequence: "the wiki still serves. The Table link
// is absent, not disabled". Nothing here disables anything — the absence of the
// Table link is the route's business, and this notice says why it is absent.
//
// Not a live region, which is the one place this state departs from §4.7's
// "both new states are assertive-adjacent". §7.5's channel table is the
// operative rule about which regions are assertive, and it names ruleset drift,
// game ended and connection lost — this notice is not among them. A persistent
// condition announced on every navigation is worse than one the reader can see,
// and this one is on the campaign overview, which is where they are already
// looking.
//
// Not colour-alone either: §6.5 requires a status to pair hue with an icon or a
// text label, and the heading is the text label.
templ SystemMissing(view SystemMissingView) {
	<section class="notice notice--warning" aria-labelledby={ headingID(systemMissingTestID) } data-testid={ systemMissingTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(systemMissingTestID), "Gameplay system not installed", view.Heading)
		<p>This campaign's gameplay system (<code data-testid="system-missing-id">{ displayText(view.SystemID, maxSystemIDDisplay) }</code>) is not installed. The wiki is unaffected.</p>
		<p>The Table is unavailable until it is installed.</p>
	</section>
}

// RulesetDriftView is the `ruleset_version` drift state.
type RulesetDriftView struct {
	// RulesHref is the campaign's Rules tab, which is where a GM resolves this.
	// Empty omits the link and leaves the refusal standing, which is the correct
	// default: refusing is a decision, not a broken control.
	RulesHref string
	// Heading is the heading level. This state is the centre of `/play`.
	Heading Heading
}

// RulesetDrift is the block on `/play` when the table's rules version does not
// match the one it was last played under.
//
// §4.7's copy is used and its framing kept: "the UI states the refusal as a
// decision, not an error", which is why the heading says what happened and the
// paragraph says what was decided. §16.5's default is refuse, so this is the
// ordinary state of a campaign whose rules changed, not an exceptional one.
//
// `role="alert"` is §4.7's own and §7.5's: a GM who expected a playable table
// must be told plainly, and the refusal is not something a polite queue behind
// two other announcements gets across.
templ RulesetDrift(view RulesetDriftView) {
	<section class="notice notice--warning" role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-labelledby={ headingID(rulesetDriftTestID) } data-testid={ rulesetDriftTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(rulesetDriftTestID), "This table was last played under a different rules version", view.Heading)
		<p data-testid="ruleset-drift-message">Resuming is blocked.</p>
		if view.RulesHref != "" {
			<a class={ TargetClass } href={ view.RulesHref } data-testid="ruleset-drift-link">Open Rules settings</a>
		}
	</section>
}

// ConnectionLostView is the lost-connection state.
type ConnectionLostView struct {
	// Heading is the heading level.
	Heading Heading
}

// ConnectionLost is the block that says the table's connection dropped.
//
// Not a toast and not a header dot. §9 says a reconnect during an open dialog
// produces "no focus change, no toast over the dialog; the header indicator is
// the only signal", and the condition persists until the socket is back — which
// is §4.7's own test for why the degraded states are blocks rather than
// announcements that fire once.
//
// `role="alert"` is §7.5's: the reader's view of the table has stopped being
// current, and a dice roll that did not arrive needs saying now. Nothing here
// moves focus, because §7.4 prohibits a background event moving it and a socket
// is the definition of one.
templ ConnectionLost(view ConnectionLostView) {
	<section class="notice notice--warning" role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-labelledby={ headingID(connectionLostTestID) } data-testid={ connectionLostTestID }>
		@stateHeading(headingID(connectionLostTestID), "The connection to this table was lost", view.Heading)
		<p>Reconnecting. Nothing you typed has been lost.</p>
	</section>
}